Non-Relational Database

A non-relational database is a type of database that does not store data in
traditional tables with rows and columns. Instead, it stores data in flexible formats
such as documents, key-value pairs, graphs, or wide-column structures.

It is commonly used when applications need to handle large amounts of unstructured
or semi-structured data and require flexible schemas.

Example: MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra, Neo4j.